    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Reverse Mortgage Costs and Fees: What They Are, How They Add Up, and How to Evaluate Them Honestly</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you're 62 or older and seriously considering a reverse mortgage, one of the most responsible things you can do before moving forward is understand exactly what it costs. The fees and interest structure of a reverse mortgage are meaningfully different from a traditional mortgage, and a homeowner who understands them clearly is in a far better position to decide whether the product makes sense than one who discovers them after the fact.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Honest Starting Point</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A reverse mortgage is not free money. It's a loan against your home equity that carries upfront costs and ongoing interest that accumulates over time. The benefit it provides (access to equity without monthly payments and without selling your home) comes at a price worth understanding before you commit. What makes a reverse mortgage worth evaluating seriously is not that the costs are trivial but that for many homeowners, the access and flexibility it provides compare favorably to the alternatives. That's the frame this article is written from.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Part That Catches People Off Guard: Interest Compounds Over Time</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's what many borrowers don't fully internalize at the outset. Because you're not making monthly payments, the interest on a reverse mortgage doesn't get paid down. It gets added to the loan balance each month. Next month, interest accrues on a larger balance. Over a long loan term, this compounding effect means the total amount owed can grow substantially beyond what was originally borrowed.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">This is not a hidden trap. It's the fundamental mechanic of the product, and it's disclosed clearly. But it means a reverse mortgage used for 20 years looks very different financially than one used for five. Understanding your likely time horizon matters, and your age at origination is one of the most important inputs in evaluating whether the math works in your favor.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Reverse Mortgage Costs Actually Break Down</h2>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Origination fee.</strong> Lenders charge an origination fee to process the loan. For HECMs, this fee is regulated by FHA and capped based on the home's value. It is typically financed into the loan rather than paid out of pocket at closing.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>FHA mortgage insurance premium.</strong> HECM borrowers pay an upfront mortgage insurance premium at closing and an ongoing annual premium added to the loan balance. This insurance is what guarantees the non-recourse protection for borrowers and heirs and it's also what ensures you can never owe more than the home is worth.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Closing costs.</strong> Standard closing costs apply, including appraisal, title insurance, recording fees, and other third-party charges. These are similar to the closing costs on a traditional mortgage.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Interest.</strong> Interest accrues on the outstanding balance over the life of the loan. Rates may be fixed or variable depending on the disbursement structure chosen. Variable rates are tied to a market index and adjust over time.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Servicing fees.</strong> Some lenders charge ongoing monthly servicing fees, though these have become less common. Ask specifically about servicing fees when comparing lenders.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">All of these costs, with the exception of ongoing interest, can typically be financed into the loan rather than paid upfront, which means you may be able to proceed with little or no out-of-pocket expense at closing. The tradeoff is a larger starting loan balance.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Request a full loan cost disclosure from any lender you're evaluating.</strong> This includes origination fees, mortgage insurance, estimated closing costs, and the projected interest rate. You are entitled to a clear itemization before you commit to anything.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask for a projected amortization schedule showing the loan balance at five, ten, and fifteen years.</strong> This is the clearest way to understand how interest compounds over your expected time in the home.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare total costs against the alternatives.</strong> The question isn't whether a reverse mortgage has costs. It does. The question is whether those costs are higher or lower than the cost of the next best alternative (whether that's selling, downsizing, taking a home equity loan, or drawing down investment assets).</li>
      <li><strong>Shop more than one lender.</strong> Origination fees and interest rates vary between lenders. The FHA caps certain fees but not all. Comparing two or three offers costs you nothing and can meaningfully affect your total loan cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the HUD counseling session to review the numbers in your specific scenario.</strong> The independent counselor can walk through the cost structure with you in detail and is required to present alternatives to the reverse mortgage before you proceed.</li>
    </ol>

